{"attributes": {"type": "DifferentialExperiment", "particle": "decay", "reaction": "PFNS", "nuclide": "Cf-252", "library": "ARIADNE", "documentation": "EXFOR No. = 31405.003. Journal article = Bao et al., 50 Years Nucl.Fission Conf., Washington D.C., 1989, Vol.2, p.951 (1989).The data by Bao et al. are in EXFOR sub-entries 31405.003. The data were published as part of proceedings~\\cite{Bao1989} after Mannhart\u2019s evaluation. Mannhart rejected those data because as is stated in Ref.~\\cite{Mannhart2008presentation}: \"The data of Bao et al.. are still quoted as preliminary and indicate difficulties in the proper correction of the lithium resonance.\" Otherwise, the data are faily well described.Bao et al. used a 0.95-cm thick (45 mm diameter) $^6$Li glass detector (NE912) to count neutrons. The neutron-detector efficiency is expilicitly given in Table 1 of Ref.~\\cite{Bao1989}; it was simulated via Monte Carlo codes and compared to Lajtai's efficiency curve~\\cite{Lajtai1990} who used the same detector type and dimensions. More recent MCNP simulations~\\cite{NeudeckerTaddeucci2019} verified the efficiencies for most energies except for 200 keV (the $^6$Li peak). There Lajtai's efficiency is more than 10\\% higher. While both simulations have limitations (missing environmental effects), it is interesting to note that Lajtai data show an increase at these energies that is not observed by Poenitz. Multiple scattering and other backgrounds were estimated by measuring with and without a Cu shadow cone. The TOF path was 30 cm long and was determined with an uncertainty of 1 mm. Fission fragments were detected in an 99.4\\% efficient mini-ionization chamber. 252Cf was on don a 0.05-mm thick Pt backing with 0.1 mm diameter; it had an activity of 3.8e+3 f/s. The time resolution was 1.8 ns.\nCorrections were undertaken for many major effects except for the shadow-bar background and finalized detector response.Detector efficient and energy uncertainties (relative to the PFNS) along with statistical uncertainties were provided in EXFOR. 1\\% background uncertainties for gammas were mentioned, 0.2\\% and 0.15\\% are assumed for random coincidences and multiple scattering based on the correction size.One major concern is how the Cu shadow bar was used. It was situated between the fission amd neutron detector without shielding. That would cause a lot of scattering that was not corrected for the background measurement. Also, Lajtai et al. and Bao et al. assumed the shadow cone to be a perfect absorber for neutrons emitted from the fission chamber. Reference~\\cite{NeudeckerTaddeucci2019} showed that this is not the case and estmated the combined effect om the PFNS to be 10\\% which we take as additional uncertainty. Another issue is that the detector response was only simulated including the material of the crystal and not the surrounding.  One concern is the pronounced Li-6 peak that affects results until 500 keV. We rejected Boldeman Li-6 data because of similar behavior.The data set is adopted for the ML analysis but not for the new standards evaluation as they are preliminary Dyachenko.", "date": "2023-08-04", "Author": ["Bao", "Tang", "Fan", "Cao"], "Year": "1989", "Facility": "N/A", "Institute": "3CPRBJG", "Isotope": "Cf-252", "Quantity": "PFNS", "MeasurementType": "shape", "ConcernEXFOR": "preliminary", "ConcernAIACHNE": ["Shadow-bar background", "multiple scattering", "detector response", "preliminary"], "ConcernAIACHNE_text": "The data are listed as preliminary because their detector efficiency is not final (not fully independently validated). One concern is the pronounced Li-6 peak that affects results until 500 keV. We rejected Boldeman Li-6 data because of similar behavior. One major concern is how the brass shadow bar was used. It was situated between the fission amd neutron detector without shielding. That would cause a lot of scattering that was not corrected for the background measurement. Also, Lajtai et al. assumed the shadow cone to be a perfect absorber for neutrons emitted from the fission chamber. Reference~\\cite{NeudeckerTaddeucci2019} showed that this is not the case and estimated the combined effect on the PFNS to be 10\\% which we take as additional uncertainty. Multiple scattweing is only approximatelty calculated for the crystal but not the surrounding. Another issue is that the detector response was only simulated including the material of the crystal and not the surrounding. Also, Fig.~2 of~\\cite{Lajtai1990} shows a smearing of the detector response to higher energies; only a  smearing to lower energies is expected. Mistakes in the response function could especially affect PFNS values above the $^6$Li resonance. ", "BackgroundCorrected": "partial", "AlphaBackground": "y", "GammaBackground": "y", "RandomCoincidenceBackground": "y", "ShadowBarBackground": "n", "WrapAroundBackground": "N/A", "MultipleScatteringSampleBackingCorrected": "y", "MultipleScatteringSurroundingCorrected": "y", "AttenuationSampleBackingCorrected": "y", "AttenuationSurroundingCorrected": "y", "FissionDetectionEfficiencyCorrected": "y", "NeutronDetectionEfficiencyCorrected": "y", "NeutronDetectionResponseCorrected": "y", "SampleDecayCorrected": "unknown", "FissionFragmentAbsorptioninSampleCorrected": "y", "SignalPulsePileupCorrected": "unknown", "DeadtimeCorrected": "unknown", "AngularDistributionFissionFragmentsCorrected": "y", "ImpuritiesCorrected": "y", "Mannhart": "rejected", "CorrelatedData": ["40875.002", "40875.003", "41158.002"], "EXFORnumber": "31405.003", "Accepted": "y", "MissingCorrections": ["ShadowBarBackground", "Deadtime"], "FissionDetector": {"Type": "IOCH (mini-ionization chamber)", "ParticleDetected": "FF", "Efficiency": 99.4, "EfficiencyMethod": "Measured (at two angles)", "FFAbsorptionAngularDistributionMethod": "Measured (at two angles)", "AngularAcceptaneofFF": "unknown", "Gas": {"Type": "unknown", "Purity": "unknown", "Pressure": "unknown"}, "Size": {"geometry": "unknown", "diameter": "unknown", "height": "unknown", "wall_thickness": "unknown"}, "NoPhotomultipliers": 1, "Photomultipliers": "ORTEC142A", "Weight": 1.6, "Weight_unit": "g", "StructuralMaterial": "unknown", "ReferenceDetector": "N/A"}, "NeutronDetector": {"Type": "GLASD (6Li)", "Number": "NE912", "ParticleDetected": "N", "Efficiency": "0.44-6.01", "ResponseDeterminationMethod": ["MC", "Calculated", "Measurement"], "EfficiencyDeterminationMethod": ["MC", "Calculated", "Measurement"], "Angle": "unknown", "TOFLength": {"Value": 30.0, "Unit": "cm", "Unc": 0.1, "Unit_Unc": "cm"}, "trsl": {"Value": 1.8, "Unit": "ns", "Measure": "1-sigma"}, "t0Determination": "Gamma peak", "Gas": {"Type": "N/A", "Purity": "N/A", "Pressure": "N/A"}, "Size": {"geometry": "cylinder", "diameter": 45, "diameter_unit": "mm", "height": 9.5, "height_unit": "mm"}, "NoPhotomultipliers": 1, "Photomultipliers": "RCA-8850", "Weight": "unknown", "StructuralMaterial": ["6Li", "Al"], "ReferenceDetector": "N/A", "ReferenceReaction": "6Li(n,a)"}, "Shielding": {"Material": "N/A", "Size": "N/A", "DistanceFromNeutronDetector": "N/A", "Purpose": "N/A"}, "Sample": {"Activity": 3800.0, "Activity_unit": "f/s", "Reused": "unknown", "Form": "unknown", "Mass": "unknown", "Diameter": "unknown", "Thickness": "unknown", "Homogenity": "unknown", "Contaminants": "<4.0e-4%", "BackingMaterial": "Stainless steel", "BackingDiameter": "unknown", "BackingThickness": 0.1, "BackingThickness_unit": "mm", "DecayDeterminationMethod": "unknown", "Configuration": "OneSample (on electrode)", "Fabrication": "transfusion (in vacuum)", "ImpurityDetermination": "unknown"}, "ShadowBar": {"Used": "y", "Material": "Cu", "Size": 20, "Size_unit": "cm", "Material of structure holding it": "unknown"}, "OutgoingEnergyDetermination": "TOF", "IndividualChannelWidthDetermination": "measurement (two gamma lines, calibrated time intervals)", "AverageChannelWidthDetermination": "measurement (two gamma lines, calibrated time intervals)", "BackgroundMethods": {"Ge